{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>And he went out, to meet his father-in-law.</b> Because of Jethro's honor and wisdom, and not for his wife and sons, for it is not the custom of an honored man to go out to meet his wife or sons:" "<b>And he bowed.</b> The grammar of וישתחו is difficult. We know that its root is among those with heh in final position, and the vav stands in place of heh, like the vav in שלו הייתי (Job 16:12), which he says of himself לא שלותי (Job 3:26). Now when they said the singular וישתחו, it should have been וישתחוה, for the tav is of the hitpa'el pattern, like והאיש משתאה לה (Genesis 24:21), for the rule of the shin-letter roots is that if one of them is peh of the verb, and the letter before them is tav of the hitpa'el, then since they replaced the heh with vav in the word וישתחו, they retained the first vav — which is not a plural marker — in place of the root heh and elided the [second] vav and said וישתחו. And because vav at the end of a word is a plural marker when it is elided, as in למה תתראו (Genesis 42:1), the word וישתחו, with elision of the vav, would have looked like a plural marker — therefore the word had to be mil'el, to distinguish between it and their convention for the plural:" "And according to reasoning, the one who goes out is the one who bows to the other." "And Scripture called him 'his friend' because of his great distinction in wisdom." "<b>And they came to the tent.</b> For it is known that it is the tent of Moses."], :book :exodus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:35:33.578280489Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}